---
import MethodologySection from "../components/MethodologySection.astro";
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import CalculatorPageShell from "../components/CalculatorPageShell.astro";
import FAQSection from "../components/FAQSection.astro";
import RelatedCalculators from "../components/RelatedCalculators.astro";
---

<Layout title="Should I Spend $10,000 on a Europe Trip?">
  <style>
    .calculator-box {
      background: white;
      border: 1px solid #d8e0ea;
      border-radius: 18px;
      padding: 1.5rem;
      margin: 2rem 0;
    }

:global(main) {
  padding-top: 3rem;
}
    .form-grid {
      display: grid;
      gap: 1rem;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(220px, 1fr));
    }

    label {
      font-weight: 700;
      display: block;
      margin-bottom: 0.35rem;
    }

    input {
      width: 100%;
      padding: 0.75rem;
      border: 1px solid #cbd5e1;
      border-radius: 10px;
      font-size: 1rem;
    }

    .helper-link {
      margin-top: 0.35rem;
      font-size: 0.9rem;
    }

    .helper-link a {
      color: #0f766e;
      font-weight: 700;
      text-decoration: none;
    }

    button {
      margin-top: 1rem;
      background: #061126;
      color: white;
      border: 0;
      padding: 0.85rem 1.15rem;
      border-radius: 10px;
      font-weight: 800;
      cursor: pointer;
    }

    .result {
      margin-top: 1.25rem;
      padding: 1rem;
      border-radius: 14px;
      background: #f1f5f9;
      display: none;
    }

    .content-section {
      margin: 2rem 0;
    }

    .content-section h2 {
      margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
    }

    .content-section p,
    .content-section li {
      line-height: 1.7;
    }
  </style>

  <CalculatorPageShell
    eyebrow="Travel Calculator"
    title="Should I Spend $10,000 on a Europe Trip?"
    description="Estimate whether a $10,000 Europe trip fits your income, savings, debt load, emergency cushion, and travel priorities."
  >
    <div class="calculator-box">
      <div class="form-grid">
        <div>
          <label for="income">Annual Household Income</label>
          <input id="income" type="number" placeholder="90000" />
        </div>

        <div>
          <label for="savings">Current Savings</label>
          <input id="savings" type="number" placeholder="25000" />
        </div>

        <div>
          <label for="vacation">Europe Trip Cost</label>
          <input id="vacation" type="number" value="10000" />
        </div>

        <div>
          <label for="debt">Total Debt</label>
          <input id="debt" type="number" placeholder="8000" />
          <p class="helper-link">
            <a href="/what-counts-as-debt">Not sure what counts as debt? Read the debt guide</a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <button id="calculateBtn">Calculate Verdict</button>

      <div id="result" class="result">
        <h2 id="verdict"></h2>
        <p id="resultText"></p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="content-section">
      <h2>What a $10,000 Europe Trip Really Costs</h2>
      <p>
        A $10,000 Europe trip can be reasonable, but only if the budget includes the full trip — not just flights and hotels.
        For many travelers, the real cost includes airfare, hotels or apartments, train tickets, local transit, meals,
        museums, tours, luggage fees, exchange-rate movement, credit card fees, and a small emergency buffer.
      </p>
      <p>
        The number also changes depending on the group. A couple spending $10,000 on a 10- to 14-day Europe trip is in a
        different position than a family trying to cover four to six people across Rome, Florence, Paris, London, Barcelona,
        Amsterdam, or the Amalfi Coast. Trip length matters just as much as destination.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="content-section">
      <h2>When a $10,000 Europe Trip Makes Sense</h2>
      <p>
        A $10,000 Europe trip makes the most sense when it comes from planned savings, does not drain your emergency fund,
        and does not force you to carry high-interest credit card debt afterward. It is also easier to justify if the trip
        covers a major anniversary, honeymoon, graduation, milestone birthday, or once-in-a-decade family experience.
      </p>
      <p>
        The strongest green-light scenario is simple: you can pay for the trip in cash, keep several months of expenses
        untouched, cover normal bills comfortably, and return home without financial stress.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="content-section">
      <h2>When You Should Wait</h2>
      <p>
  Waiting may be smarter if the trip would drain savings, require
  <a href="/how-to-avoid-vacation-debt">vacation debt</a>,
  delay debt payoff, or make normal bills feel tight after you return home.
</p>
      <p>
        Waiting can also improve the trip itself. More savings may let you book better flight times, stay closer to city
        centers, avoid rushed train connections, and build in experiences that make the trip feel complete instead of
        financially cramped.
      </p>
    </div>

    <FAQSection
      faqs={[
        {
          question: "Is $10,000 enough for a Europe trip?",
          answer:
            "Yes, $10,000 can be enough for a Europe trip, especially for a couple or a carefully planned family trip. The answer depends on airfare, hotel quality, trip length, cities visited, train costs, meals, tours, and how much buffer you keep for surprises.",
        },
        {
          question: "Is a $10,000 Europe trip too expensive?",
          answer:
            "A $10,000 Europe trip is too expensive if it creates credit card debt, drains your emergency fund, or makes normal bills stressful. It can be reasonable if it is saved for in advance and fits comfortably within your income and savings.",
        },
        {
          question: "Should I use savings or finance a Europe trip?",
          answer:
            "Savings are usually the better choice for a Europe trip. Financing a vacation with high-interest debt can make the trip cost far more than the sticker price and create stress after you return.",
        },
        {
          question: "What should be included in a Europe trip budget?",
          answer:
            "A Europe trip budget should include flights, hotels, trains, local transportation, meals, museums, tours, travel insurance, luggage fees, exchange-rate buffer, phone data, souvenirs, and emergency money.",
        },
        <p>
  A Europe trip also creates different financial pressures than a
  <a href="/should-i-spend-8000-on-a-disney-vacation">Disney vacation</a>
  or a
  <a href="/should-i-spend-12000-on-a-cruise">cruise</a>.
  Flights, trains, hotels, exchange-rate swings, and multi-city transportation
  can make international travel harder to predict financially.
</p>
      ]}
    />
<MethodologySection
  category="Europe travel spending"
  updated="May 2026"
/>

    <RelatedCalculators
      calculators={[
        {
          category: "Travel",
          title: "Should I Spend $5,000 on a Vacation?",
          href: "/should-i-spend-5000-on-a-vacation",
        },
        {
          category: "Travel",
          title: "Should I Spend $8,000 on a Disney Vacation?",
          href: "/should-i-spend-8000-on-a-disney-vacation",
        },
        {
          category: "Travel",
          title: "Should I Spend $12,000 on a Cruise?",
          href: "/should-i-spend-12000-on-a-cruise",
        },
        {
          category: "Travel",
          title: "Browse Travel Calculators",
          href: "/travel-calculators",
        },
      ]}
    />
  </CalculatorPageShell>

  <script is:inline>
    function calculateVacation() {
      const income = Number(document.getElementById("income").value);
      const savings = Number(document.getElementById("savings").value);
      const vacation = Number(document.getElementById("vacation").value);
      const debt = Number(document.getElementById("debt").value);

      let score = 100;

      const savingsImpact = savings > 0 ? (vacation / savings) * 100 : 100;
      const incomeImpact = income > 0 ? (vacation / income) * 100 : 100;
      const debtRatio = income > 0 ? (debt / income) * 100 : 50;

      score -= savingsImpact * 0.22;
      score -= incomeImpact * 0.9;
      score -= debtRatio * 0.18;

      const verdict = document.getElementById("verdict");
      const resultText = document.getElementById("resultText");
      const result = document.getElementById("result");

      result.style.display = "block";

      if (!income || !savings || !vacation) {
        verdict.textContent = "Add your numbers first";
        resultText.textContent =
          "Enter your income, savings, estimated Europe trip cost, and debt so the calculator can give a useful verdict.";
        return;
      }

      if (score >= 75) {
        verdict.textContent = "This Europe trip looks financially reasonable";
        resultText.textContent =
          "A $10,000 Europe trip appears manageable based on your income, savings, and debt. Make sure your budget includes flights, hotels, trains, meals, tours, exchange-rate movement, and an emergency cushion.";
      } else if (score >= 50) {
        verdict.textContent = "This Europe trip is possible, but plan carefully";
        resultText.textContent =
          "The trip may be doable, but the budget deserves caution. Look for ways to reduce airfare, shorten the trip, choose fewer cities, use trains strategically, or build more savings before booking.";
      } else {
        verdict.textContent = "You may want to wait before spending $10,000";
        resultText.textContent =
          "This trip could put too much pressure on your savings, debt load, or monthly flexibility. Consider delaying the trip, lowering the budget, or saving more before committing.";
      }
    }

    document.getElementById("calculateBtn").addEventListener("click", calculateVacation);
  </script>
</Layout>